
---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/home.css";
---

<BaseLayout title="Home">
  <main class="home-main">

    <!-- CINEMATIC OPENING -->
    <section class="video-hero">
      <video
        class="hero-video"
        autoplay
        muted
        loop
        playsinline
        preload="auto"
        poster="/videos/larvalfishfin-regeneration-poster.jpg"
      >
        <source
          src="/videos/larvalfishfin-regeneration.mp4"
          type="video/mp4"
        />
      </video>

      <div class="hero-overlay">
        <h1>Physics of Regenerative Soft Matter</h1>
        <p>
          DISCOVERING THE LATENT, ELECTRICAL VARIABLES OF LIFE
        </p>
      </div>

      <a href="#mission" class="scroll-prompt">
        <span>Our Mission</span>
        <span class="arrow">↓</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    <!-- MISSION STATEMENT -->
    <section id="mission" class="mission-section">
      <div class="mission-content">
        <p class="eyebrow">
          DISCOVERING THE LATENT, ELECTRICAL VARIABLES OF LIFE
        </p>

        <h2>Physics of Regenerative Soft Matter</h2>

        <p>
          How does a tissue know what to rebuild? An amputated fin grows back to
          the size it was, so the cells that remain must hold information about
          the missing structure, read it out, and act on it. Other tissues only
          close the wound. We ask where that information sits, how few variables
          carry it, and what happens when we change them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Development has its canonical carriers: morphogen gradients and
          patterns of gene expression. Cells also maintain charge distributions
          and ionic currents — collective states, held away from equilibrium by
          ion channels and pumps that work continuously to sustain them. These
          carry a further layer of information, electrical rather than molecular,
          and one whose content is still largely unread. It sets the size an organ
          reaches, changes how much of it is rebuilt after injury, and can
          separate development from regeneration. A single change in how a
          zebrafish fin handles potassium leaves its development intact and takes
          away its capacity to regrow.
        </p>

        <p>
          Watching a tissue cannot separate the variable that decides an outcome
          from the many that merely accompany it. So we close the loop: we impose
          a perturbation — a channel opened with light, a bath shifted, a field
          applied — read the electrical and molecular state that follows, and
          commit to a prediction before the tissue rebuilds. The regeneration
          outcome judges the model. Theory and image-based machine learning tell
          us which few variables carry the prediction, and which intervention is
          worth making next to read out the information essential to build an
          organ, or an organ on a chip, to the right shape and size.
        </p>

        <div class="mission-actions">
  <a class="mission-button research-button" href="/research">
    Explore Research
    <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
  </a>

  <a class="mission-button research-button" href="/publications">
    Check Publications
    <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
  </a>

  <a class="mission-button recruiting-button" href="/join">
    We Are Recruiting
    <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
  </a>
</div>

      </div>
      <a href="#lab-news" class="scroll-prompt mission-scroll-prompt">
        <span>Check News</span>
        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    
<!-- RESEARCH THEMES + LAB NEWS -->
<section class="home-updates" id="lab-news">

  
<!-- LEFT: RESEARCH THEMES -->
<div class="research-column">
  <p class="section-eyebrow">WHAT WE EXPLORE</p>
  <h2>Research Themes</h2>

  <div class="research-list">

    <article class="research-item">
      <span class="item-number">01</span>
      <h3>Electrical states and tissue function</h3>
      <p>
        A tissue’s electrical state can be measured and manipulated. What it
        will then do is much harder to say: tissue behaviour is high-dimensional,
        and most of those dimensions turn out not to matter. We ask how few
        electrical and ionic variables are enough to predict a functional
        outcome — whether a fin regrows to size, whether a planarian fragment
        rebuilds a head — using channel mutants and ionic conditions as the
        axes, and testing sufficiency by prediction.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="research-item">
      <span class="item-number">02</span>
      <h3>The kinetics of regeneration</h3>
      <p>
        Regeneration is a race: a tissue must rebuild before the window for
        rebuilding closes. Rates and windows differ between tissues, between
        mutants, and between animals — and that variation is a natural
        experiment, one that lets us ask what the constraint really is and why
        evolution settled where it did. We ask what sets the rate and the
        window, and whether a tissue that fails to regenerate is one that
        cannot, or one that ran out of time.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="research-item">
      <span class="item-number">03</span>
      <h3>Programmable morphogenesis</h3>
      <p>
        Tissues are usually steered by morphogens, by geometry, or by mechanics.
        We ask whether the same can be done electrically: whether setting channel
        activity, ionic conditions and applied fields in space and time is
        enough to specify what a tissue builds — first in regenerating fins,
        later in organoids and on chips.
      </p>
    </article>

  </div>

  <a class="section-link" href="/research">
    Explore Our Research ↗
  </a>
</div>

  <!-- RIGHT: LAB NEWS -->
  <div class="news-column">
    <p class="section-eyebrow">LATEST UPDATES</p>
    <h2>Lab News</h2>

    <div class="news-list">
      <article class="news-item recruitment">
        <span class="news-date">RECRUITMENT</span>
        <h3>Join the Liu Lab</h3>
        <p>
          We welcome motivated researchers interested
          in the physics of living systems.
        </p>
        <h4 class="news-subhead">Who we are looking for</h4>
        <p>
          Students and postdocs from physics, biology, engineering or
          computation. You do not need a background in bioelectricity. If you
          are drawn to how organs rebuild themselves, to nonequilibrium and
          complex systems, to quantitative imaging, or to building the
          instrument that makes a measurement possible, there is a project here
          for you. If you already work on bioelectricity, so much the better —
          it is the variable we know best, and the one we push hardest.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write with a paragraph on what you find interesting and why. That
          matters more than a matching CV.
        </p>
        <a href="/join">
          How to Join ↗
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="news-item">
        <span class="news-date">COMING SOON</span>
        <h3>Physics of Regenerative Soft Matter Lab @Westlake_CIS</h3>
        <p>
          In September 2027, we will launch at the Center for
          Interdisciplinary Studies!
          </p>

          <p>

          PhD students from all Schools are welcomed to get in touch.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="news-item">
        <span class="news-date">NITMB SCHOLAR-IN-RESIDENCE</span>
        <h3>Image-Based Scientific Machine Learning</h3>
        <p>
          Jinghui has been selected as a scholar-in-residence for the NITMB Long
          Program “Image-Based Scientific Machine Learning for Theories of
          Biological Dynamics Across Scales,” running 1 February to 12 March 2027.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://www.nitmb.org/event/image-based-scientific-machine-learning-for-theories-of-biological-dynamics-across-scales/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          About the program ↗
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="news-item">
        <span class="news-date">PHYSIKERIN DER WOCHE</span>
        <h3>Featured by the German Physical Society</h3>
        <p>
          Jinghui will be featured as the woman physicist in the German
          Physical Society’s “Physikerin der Woche” series, in calendar week 2
          of 2027. The series is run by the DPG’s Equal Opportunities Working
          Group and spotlights women physicists based in Germany.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://www.dpg-physik.de/vereinigungen/fachuebergreifend/ak/akc/publikationen/physikerin-der-woche"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          About the series ↗
        </a>
      </article>

    </div>
  </div>

</section>


    </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // Some browsers (e.g. Safari) hold back autoplay; start it explicitly,
  // and retry on the first interaction if the browser still blocks it.
  const video = document.querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>(".hero-video");
  if (video) {
    video.muted = true;
    const tryPlay = () => video.play().catch(() => {});
    tryPlay();
    video.addEventListener("canplay", tryPlay, { once: true });
    const onFirstInteraction = () => {
      tryPlay();
      window.removeEventListener("pointerdown", onFirstInteraction);
      window.removeEventListener("keydown", onFirstInteraction);
      window.removeEventListener("scroll", onFirstInteraction);
    };
    window.addEventListener("pointerdown", onFirstInteraction);
    window.addEventListener("keydown", onFirstInteraction);
    window.addEventListener("scroll", onFirstInteraction, { passive: true });
  }
</script>
